.TH TH8_RE_SYNTAX 1 "June 22, 2026" "TH8" "TH8 Regular Expression Syntax"
.SH NAME
th8_re_syntax \- regular expression syntax used by TH8
.SH DESCRIPTION
TH8 uses the PostgreSQL/Spencer Advanced Regular Expression (ARE)
engine, adapted for safe embedding.  This engine provides a superset
of POSIX Extended Regular Expressions (ERE) with additional features
including non-greedy quantifiers, look-ahead, look-behind,
back-references, and character classes.
.PP
The
.B regexp
and
.B regsub
commands, and the
.B "switch \-regexp"
option, all use this syntax.
.PP
The engine operates in
.B REG_ADVANCED
mode by default.
.SH "BASIC SYNTAX"
.SS "Atoms"
.TP
.I c
A single ordinary character matches itself.
.TP
.B .
Matches any single character (except newline in certain modes).
.TP
.BI [ chars ]
A bracket expression matches any one of the enclosed characters.
.TP
.BI [^ chars ]
A negated bracket expression matches any character NOT in the set.
.TP
.B ^
Matches the beginning of the string (or line in
.B \-lineanchor
mode).
.TP
.B $
Matches the end of the string (or line in
.B \-lineanchor
mode).
.TP
.BI ( re )
Capturing group.  Matches
.I re
and stores the matched text for back-reference and extraction.
.TP
.BI (?: re )
Non-capturing group.  Groups
.I re
for quantification but does not capture.
.SS "Quantifiers"
.TP
.B *
Matches the preceding atom zero or more times (greedy).
.TP
.B +
Matches the preceding atom one or more times (greedy).
.TP
.B ?
Matches the preceding atom zero or one time (greedy).
.TP
.BI { n }
Matches the preceding atom exactly
.I n
times.
.TP
.BI { n ,}
Matches the preceding atom
.I n
or more times.
.TP
.BI { n , m }
Matches the preceding atom at least
.I n
and at most
.I m
times.
.SS "Non-Greedy Quantifiers"
Appending
.B ?
to any quantifier makes it non-greedy (matches as few
characters as possible):
.BR *? ", " +? ", " ?? ", " { n , m }? .
.SS "Alternation"
.TP
.IB re1 | re2
Matches either
.I re1
or
.IR re2 .
.SH "CHARACTER CLASSES"
Within bracket expressions
.RB ( [ ] ),
the following special forms are recognized:
.SS "POSIX Classes"
.TP
.B [:alnum:]
Alphanumeric characters.
.TP
.B [:alpha:]
Alphabetic characters.
.TP
.B [:digit:]
Decimal digits.
.TP
.B [:upper:]
Uppercase letters.
.TP
.B [:lower:]
Lowercase letters.
.TP
.B [:space:]
Whitespace characters (space, tab, newline, etc.).
.TP
.B [:punct:]
Punctuation characters.
.TP
.B [:graph:]
Visible characters (non-space printable).
.TP
.B [:print:]
Printable characters (visible + space).
.TP
.B [:cntrl:]
Control characters.
.TP
.B [:xdigit:]
Hexadecimal digits.
.TP
.B [:blank:]
Horizontal whitespace (space and tab only).
.SS "Ranges"
.TP
.IB a \- z
Matches any character in the range from
.I a
to
.IR z .
.SS "Special Characters in Brackets"
.TP
.B ]
As the first character after
.B [
or
.BR [^ ,
matches a literal
.BR ] .
.TP
.B \-
As the first or last character, matches a literal
.BR \- .
.SH "ESCAPE SEQUENCES"
Within an ARE, the following backslash escapes are recognized:
.SS "Character Escapes"
.TP
.B \ea
Alert (bell, 0x07).
.TP
.B \eb
Backspace (0x08).  Only inside bracket expressions.
.TP
.B \ef
Form feed (0x0C).
.TP
.B \en
Newline (0x0A).
.TP
.B \er
Carriage return (0x0D).
.TP
.B \et
Horizontal tab (0x09).
.TP
.B \ev
Vertical tab (0x0B).
.TP
.BI \e x hh
Hexadecimal character (1\(en2 hex digits).
.TP
.BI \e u hhhh
Unicode character (4 hex digits).
.TP
.BI \e U hhhhhhhh
Unicode character (8 hex digits).
.SS "Character Class Escapes"
.TP
.B \ed
Digit (equivalent to
.BR [[:digit:]] ).
.TP
.B \eD
Non-digit.
.TP
.B \es
Whitespace (equivalent to
.BR [[:space:]] ).
.TP
.B \eS
Non-whitespace.
.TP
.B \ew
Word character (equivalent to
.BR [[:alnum:]_] ).
.TP
.B \eW
Non-word character.
.SS "Constraint Escapes"
.TP
.B \eA
Matches only at the start of the string
(unlike
.BR ^ ,
not affected by
.BR \-lineanchor ).
.TP
.B \eZ
Matches only at the end of the string.
.TP
.B \em
Matches at the beginning of a word.
.TP
.B \eM
Matches at the end of a word.
.TP
.B \ey
Matches at a word boundary.
.TP
.B \eY
Matches at a non-word-boundary.
.SS "Back-References"
.TP
.BI \e n
Matches the same text that was matched by the
.IR n th
capturing group (1 through 9).
.SH "ADVANCED FEATURES"
.SS "Look-Ahead"
.TP
.BI (?= re )
Positive look-ahead: asserts that
.I re
matches at the current position without consuming characters.
.TP
.BI (?! re )
Negative look-ahead: asserts that
.I re
does NOT match at the current position.
.SS "Look-Behind"
.TP
.BI (?<= re )
Positive look-behind: asserts that
.I re
matches immediately before the current position without
consuming characters.
.TP
.BI (?<! re )
Negative look-behind: asserts that
.I re
does NOT match immediately before the current position.
.SS "Embedded Options"
.TP
.BI (? flags )
Enables or disables options inline.  Supported flags:
.BR i " (case-insensitive),"
.BR c " (case-sensitive, overrides "
.BR i "),"
.BR n " (newline-sensitive matching),"
.BR m " (same as " n "),"
.BR p " (partial newline-sensitive),"
.BR q " (literal/quoted mode),"
.BR s " (non-newline-sensitive),"
.BR t " (tight/non-expanded mode),"
.BR w " (inverse partial newline-sensitive),"
.BR x " (expanded mode)."
.SH "MATCHING MODES"
The following
.B regexp
and
.B regsub
switches control matching behavior:
.TP
.B \-nocase
Case-insensitive matching.
.TP
.B \-expanded
Enable expanded syntax: unescaped whitespace and
.B #
comments are ignored in the pattern.  Equivalent to the
.B (?x)
embedded option.
.TP
.B \-line
Shorthand for
.B \-linestop
and
.BR \-lineanchor .
.TP
.B \-linestop
The
.B .
and
.B [^...]
constructs do not match newline.
.TP
.B \-lineanchor
The
.B ^
and
.B $
anchors match after and before embedded newlines,
not just at the start and end of the string.
.TP
.BI \-start " n"
Begin matching at byte offset
.I n
in the string.
.TP
.B \-all
.RB ( regexp )
Count all non-overlapping matches.
.RB ( regsub )
Replace all matches.
.TP
.B \-inline
.RB ( regexp " only)"
Return matches as a list instead of storing in variables.
.TP
.B \-indices
.RB ( regexp " only)"
Store match start/end byte offsets instead of matched text.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of switches.  Required if the pattern starts with
.BR \- .
.SH "REPLACEMENT SYNTAX (regsub)"
In the
.I replacement
string of
.BR regsub :
.TP
.B &
The entire text matched by the pattern.
.TP
.BI \e n
The text matched by the
.IR n th
capturing group (0\(en9).
.B \e0
is the same as
.BR & .
.TP
.B \e\e
A literal backslash.
.SH "ENGINE DETAILS"
TH8 uses the Henry Spencer regular expression library
(PostgreSQL variant) compiled for 32-bit character (chr) width.
All strings are converted from UTF-8 to 32-bit code points before
matching and converted back after.
.PP
The engine supports:
.IP \(bu 2
POSIX Extended Regular Expressions (ERE)
.IP \(bu 2
Advanced Regular Expressions (ARE) with non-greedy quantifiers,
back-references, look-ahead, constraint escapes, and embedded options
.IP \(bu 2
Unicode character classes via POSIX bracket notation
.PP
The regex module is optional and enabled at compile time with
.BR TH8_ENABLE_REGEXP .
Memory allocation is routed through
.BR Th8_Malloc / Th8_Free
via the platform abstraction, and long-running matches can be
cancelled via
.B Th8_CancelEval
(checked via the
.B REG_CANCEL
mechanism).
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.TP
.B regexp {^[0-9]+$} "12345"
Matches a string of all digits.  Returns 1.
.TP
.B "regexp \-nocase {^hello} \(dqHELLO WORLD\(dq"
Case-insensitive match.  Returns 1.
.TP
.B "regexp {([a\-z]+)([0\-9]+)} \(dqabc123\(dq all letters digits"
Captures: \fIall\fR=\fBabc123\fR, \fIletters\fR=\fBabc\fR,
\fIdigits\fR=\fB123\fR.
.TP
.B "regsub \-all {[0\-9]+} \(dqa1b2c3\(dq N result"
Replaces all digit runs with N.  \fIresult\fR=\fBaNbNcN\fR.
.TP
.B "switch \-regexp $str { {^[0\-9]+$} {puts digit} default {puts other} }"
Branch on regex match.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR th8 (1),
.BR regexp (1tcl),
.BR re_syntax (1tcl)
